              %li
                %a{:href => "#sequence_spaces"} Sequence spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong sequence
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} domain
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            (or more generally a subset of the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-infinite+sequence"} bi-infinite sequence
            and the further generalizations
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Generalizations"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %strong infinite sequence
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lists"} lists
            in computer science. (In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+subset"} decidable subset
            of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            Sequences may also be indicated by functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \to X
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is the first countably infinite
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            relevant for the study of sequences, particularly in analysis, is the order structure.
          %p
            Up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domains"} domains
            are those of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi i
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{i\colon \mathbb{Z} \;|\; 0 \leq i \lt n\}
          %p
            for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots, \infty
          %p
            An alternative generalisation takes the domain to be a set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ord
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi i
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{i\colon \Ord \;|\; i \lt \alpha\}
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            some specific ordinal number (or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ord
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Ord
            of all ordinal numbers, if one wishes to allow for a proper class).
          %p
            A
            %em subsequence
            of a sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a_n: \mathbb{N} \to X
            is a composition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi i
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi a
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \stackrel{i}{\to} \mathbb{N} \stackrel{a}{\to} X
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is an order-preserving monomorphism. The salient point is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofinal+diagram"} cofinal
            as an embedding.
          %h2#notation Notation
          %p
            One normally writes the value of the sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            at the argument
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            rather than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(i)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a[i]
            with the free variable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a[i])_{i \lt n}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a[i]\}_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_i)
            be a sequence.’ even though ‘Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            be a sequence.’ would be less of an abuse of notation. This is all because notation for sequences arose before
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            were considered in their full generality, and one distinguished a ‘sequence’ (whose domain was a set of integers) from a ‘function’ (whose domain was an interval in the real line or a region in the complex plane). Early mathematicians also often conflated the sequence (the function itself) with its range (a subset of the function's
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/families"} families
            with index sets other than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %h2#Generalization Generalisations
          %h3#nets Nets
          %p
            Infinite sequences are often used in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
            = succeed "." do
              %em Moore–Smith sequences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            by any arbitrary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction"} directed set
          %h3#sequential_nets Sequential nets
          %p
            Recall that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            is a rather weak version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            that is accepted even in most schools of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of some widely studied
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+of+sheaves"} topos of sheaves
            over the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "." do
              %em sequential nets
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+net"} sequential net
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-valued+function"} multi-valued function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            (or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+subset"} decidable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            thereof) to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ←
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \leftarrow A \rightarrow X
            where the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to \mathbb{N}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            (or has a decidable range). Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            inherits the structure of a directed set via
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to X
            is a net. As a net, every sequential net is equivalent (in the sense of corresponding to the same
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
          %p
            (Using a multi-valued function here is a special case of an alternative definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
            that uses only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partially+ordered"} partially ordered
            directed sets; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong presequence
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prefunction"} prefunction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            or a decidable subset thereof.)
          %p
            Without WCC, many of the usual properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            and other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spaces"} sequential spaces
            fail, but they continue to hold using sequential nets in the place of sequences. For example, every (located Dedekind)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            may be represented as a sequential Cauchy net, even when they might not all be represented as Cauchy sequences; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+number"} Cauchy real number
          %h2#sequence_types Sequence types
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to A
          %p Formation rules for sequence types:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
